# Real AWS cost — ask first

**Never** create, update, or leave running resources in a **real** AWS account (CLI, console automation, SDK, CloudFormation, etc.) without the user **explicitly approving that action in the current turn**.

This includes “cheap” or free-tier-ish things (SSM Parameter, empty S3 bucket, tiny SNS topic, CFN stack with only metadata). **$0–$1 × many still counts** — do not batch unapproved creates.

## Allowed without asking

- **Local** awsbox / LocalEmu / SeaweedFS / Docker (`--profile local`, `:4566`, `:8333`)
- **Read-only** on real AWS when already authenticated for viewing UI (list/describe/screenshot) — no Create / Update / Delete / purchase

## Before any real-AWS write

1. State exactly what will be created (resource types + region)
2. Estimate cost — **including residuals after teardown** (see below)
3. Say how it will be deleted afterward
4. **Wait for explicit yes** (“go ahead”, “approved”, etc.)

If unsure whether an account is real vs local — treat as real and ask.

## Cost estimate must include residuals

“Runs for 5 minutes then delete” is not enough. Call out anything that **keeps billing after the script exits**:

| Check | Examples |
|-------|----------|
| **Min deletion / pending window** | KMS CMK `schedule-key-deletion` **7-day minimum** (prorated ~$1/mo until gone) |
| **Retention / cooldown locks** | Secrets recovery window; RDS final snapshot; OpenSearch / ElastiCache cooldown |
| **Min billable period** | Some offerings bill a day/month even if deleted early — verify before create |
| **Versioning / incomplete multipart** | S3 version stacks, MPU parts if abort fails |
| **Log / metric retention** | CW Logs groups with retention >0; unused alarms |
| **Idle provisioned capacity** | Kinesis shards, NAT GW, EIP, Load Balancers until delete completes |

State the **worst-case residual**: resource type, how long it can keep costing, and approx $. If the only clean exit still leaves a multi-day charge (KMS 7d, etc.), say that **before** asking for yes — don’t discover it after a green run.

Prefer designs that avoid sticky costs when a free/ephemeral alternative exists (e.g. skip a dedicated CMK if an AWS-managed key suffices for the assertion).
